  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Time Shifts of Exponential Signals",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert delayed and advanced exponential signals by substituting t - T or t + T into $e^{st}$ and factoring the shift into the constant multiplier $e^{-sT}$ or $e^{sT}$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Time Shifts of Exponential Signals",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Time Shifts of Exponential Signals",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For an exponential signal like $e^{at}$ or $e^{st}$, a time shift changes only the constant multiplier. The time-dependent exponential shape stays the same, so the first thing to look for is whether the input has become $t-T$ or $t+T$ inside the exponent.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{a(t-T)} = e^{at-aT} = e^{-aT}e^{at}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the delay case, distribute the exponent first, then separate the part that still depends on $t$ from the part that does not. The delayed exponential is just the original exponential multiplied by the constant factor $e^{-aT}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{a(t+T)} = e^{at+aT} = e^{aT}e^{at}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The advance case works the same way, except the constant factor has the opposite sign in the exponent. So a delay gives $e^{-aT}$ and an advance gives $e^{aT}$, while the exponential part in $t$ remains unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{s(t-T)} = e^{-sT}e^{st}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The same algebra works for $e^{st}$ with complex $s$ as well. This is the reusable rule for the rest of the lesson: substitute $t-T$ or $t+T$ into the exponential, distribute the exponent, and pull the shift out as a constant multiplier.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Time Shifts of Exponential Signals",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Delaying a Real Exponential Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)=e^{3t}$, find the delayed signal $x(t-2)$ in simplified form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the delay rule from the introduction and substitute $t-2$ into the original exponential $x(t)=e^{3t}$. The cue is the shift inside the input, and the goal is to rewrite the result as a constant times the same exponential shape.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t-2)=e^{3(t-2)}=e^{3t-6}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now separate the time-dependent part from the constant part. The $3t$ term stays inside the exponential shape, and the $-6$ term becomes a constant multiplier outside.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t-2)=e^{-6}e^{3t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the delayed signal is $e^{-6}e^{3t}$. The delay changes only the constant factor in front; it does not change the exponential dependence on $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Time Shifts of Exponential Signals",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Advancing a Real Exponential Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)=e^{3t}$, find the advanced signal $x(t+2)$ in simplified form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the advance rule from the introduction and substitute $t+2$ into the original exponential $x(t)=e^{3t}$. The cue is the shift inside the input, and the goal is to rewrite the result as a constant times the same exponential shape.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t+2)=e^{3(t+2)}=e^{3t+6}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now separate the time-dependent part from the constant part. The $3t$ term stays inside the exponential shape, and the $+6$ term becomes a constant multiplier outside.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t+2)=e^{6}e^{3t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the advanced signal is $e^{6}e^{3t}$. The advance changes only the constant factor in front; it does not change the exponential dependence on $t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Time Shifts of Exponential Signals",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Factoring a Shift in $e^{st}$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $e^{s(t-T)}$ in the form $C e^{st}$ and identify $C$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the shifted exponential $e^{s(t-T)}$ and treat the shift as part of the exponent. The cue is the form $t-T$, and the goal is to separate the $t$-dependent factor from the constant factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{s(t-T)}=e^{st-sT}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now split the exponent into a time term and a shift term. The $st$ part stays with $e^{st}$, while the $-sT$ part becomes the constant multiplier.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{s(t-T)}=e^{-sT}e^{st}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the expression is $C e^{st}$ with $C=e^{-sT}$. This is the general delay identity, and the matching advance form is $e^{s(t+T)}=e^{sT}e^{st}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Time Shifts of Exponential Signals",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Shifting Damped or Growing Sinusoids",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)=e^{(-1+j4)t}$, write the delayed signal $x(t-2)$ in factored form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same shift rule with the complex exponential $x(t)=e^{(-1+j4)t}$. The cue is the delay $t-2$, and the goal is to factor the result into a constant multiplier times the original time dependence.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t-2)=e^{(-1+j4)(t-2)}=e^{(-1+j4)t+2-j8}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now separate the constant part from the $t$-dependent part. The $(-1+j4)t$ term stays with the original complex exponential, and the $2-j8$ term becomes a multiplier outside.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t-2)=e^{2-j8}e^{(-1+j4)t}=e^{2}e^{-j8}e^{(-1+j4)t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the delayed signal is $e^{2}e^{-j8}e^{(-1+j4)t}$. The shift changes only the constant amplitude and phase factors; it does not change the exponential's time dependence.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
